---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Hale from '../components/Hale.astro';

const reject = `// a model writes this:
locus Reader {
    bus { publish Frame; }
}
// hale check:
//   error: publish references unknown topic 'Frame'
//   (no 'topic Frame' declaration in this file)`;

const mechanisms = [
  ['ASCII-only, names not symbols', 'No symbol-input or tokenizer friction. The source is exactly what the model emits.'],
  ['One primitive + a 6-pattern catalog', 'Tiny surface area to get wrong. “Idiomatic” is bounded and nameable.'],
  ['Explicit named commitments', 'contract, fallible, placement, alias-required imports — intent is on the surface, never inferred.'],
  ['Strict structural checks', 'A hallucinated field, topic, or return type is a hard error, not a silent Unknown.'],
  ['An exhaustive, groundable spec', 'The model cites the rule instead of guessing it.'],
  ['Deterministic, legible failures', 'Easy to reason about — and to self-correct from a diagnostic.'],
];
---
<Base title="Hale for agents — written by humans and LLMs">
  <section class="container prose">
    <span class="tag">/agents</span>
    <h1>Built to be written by models, too.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Hale is designed so a model can write correct, idiomatic code — and the
      compiler proves it did. Not “AI-native” as a slogan: authorship ergonomics
      plus verifiability, demonstrated by how real Hale systems already ship.
    </p>

    <h2>The method</h2>
    <p>
      Sophisticated Hale isn't written by pasting a model into a void. It's a
      three-part loop, and the website ships each piece as a reusable artifact.
    </p>

    <h3>1 · A short load-bearing rules doc</h3>
    <p>
      A handful of project-level rules the agent reads <em>before writing any
      locus</em> — the two-channel discipline, the six-pattern catalog, the
      gotchas. Drop one into your project (a future <code>hale init --agent</code>
      will scaffold it for you):
    </p>
    <p>
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/agent/AGENTS.md" download>AGENTS.md</a>
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/agent/CLAUDE.md" download>CLAUDE.md</a>
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/agent/.cursorrules" download>.cursorrules</a>
    </p>

    <h3>2 · A living friction log</h3>
    <p>
      A running file the agent reads and <strong>appends to</strong> when it hits a
      wall, so the project accumulates hard-won lessons instead of re-learning
      them. Friction discipline is a first-class agent practice — the log is the
      project's memory.
    </p>

    <h3 id="mcp">3 · The compiler as the oracle</h3>
    <p>
      <code>hale check</code> over the <strong>MCP server</strong> returns
      structured diagnostics, so the model writes → compiles → reads the error →
      fixes, in-loop. The type checker catches the mistakes models actually make:
    </p>
    <Hale code={reject} title="hale-check" />
    <p class="muted" style="font-size:0.92rem;">
      Unknown fields, unknown topics, and methods that return a managed entity are
      all hard errors — the hallucination-class bugs, stopped at build time.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>hale-mcp</strong> exposes the toolchain as MCP tools —
      <code>hale_check</code> / <code>hale_build</code> / <code>hale_run</code> /
      <code>hale_fmt</code> / <code>hale_fetch</code> and
      <code>hale_docs_search</code> (grep the spec to ground a rule) — so an agent
      compiles and verifies Hale without leaving the loop.
    </p>
    <div class="install" style="margin-bottom:0.6rem;">
      <span class="sigil">$</span>
      <span class="cmd">claude mcp add hale -- node /path/to/hale-mcp/src/index.js</span>
    </div>
    <p class="muted" style="font-size:0.92rem;">
      <a href="https://github.com/hale-lang/hale-mcp">github.com/hale-lang/hale-mcp</a>
      — set <code>HALE_BIN</code> to your <code>hale</code> binary; works with
      Claude Code, Claude Desktop, and Cursor.
    </p>

    <h2 id="context-pack">The context pack</h2>
    <p>
      A single file (essentials + the full language spec + real-world gotchas)
      that makes an assistant fluent in Hale. The <strong>slim</strong> variant
      (~14k tokens) is the essentials plus the two highest-signal spec docs — it
      fits comfortably in a context window; the <strong>full</strong> variant is
      the complete corpus.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/hale-context-slim.txt" download>Context pack — slim</a>
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/hale-context.txt" download>Full pack</a>
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/llms-full.txt" download>llms-full.txt</a>
    </p>
    <div class="install" style="margin:1rem 0 1.5rem;">
      <span class="sigil">$</span>
      <span class="cmd">curl -fsSL https://hale-lang.org/hale-context-slim.txt -o hale-context.txt</span>
    </div>

    <h2>Why the language helps</h2>
    <table class="matrix">
      <thead><tr><th>Mechanism</th><th>Why it helps a model</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {mechanisms.map(([m, w]) => (
          <tr><td><strong>{m}</strong></td><td>{w}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Use it now</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/llms.txt">llms.txt</a> / <a href="/llms-full.txt">llms-full.txt</a> — machine-readable doc index + corpus.</li>
      <li>Playground “send to assistant” export — copy a snippet with its context.</li>
      <li>Three steps: install the context pack → add a rules file → connect the MCP server.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</Base>
